  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B001",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of magic-wavelength optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B023"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B013 to B001. The defect-mediated theory of two-dimensional melting remains an exceptionally elegant and universal theoretical achievement, but the discussion makes clearer that B001 joins a sharply identifiable physical mechanism to sustained, independent experimental realization and mature precision-measurement uses. The magic-wavelength principle is a clean solution to the tension between confinement and an undisturbed clock transition. Katori's foundational concept and Ye's decisive experimental development provide a compact division of credit. This is not an award for a general improvement in timekeeping, but for the distinctive lattice-clock principle and its realization.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains the central reservation. Earlier optical-frequency metrology and later contributions by many clock groups must not be absorbed into the cited achievement. The final historical review must test whether Katori and Ye alone fairly delimit the foundational conceptual and experimental work. B013 retains greater conceptual universality, while B023 remains a powerful and experimentally consequential many-body framework; neither, however, presently combines B001's sharply defined mechanism, mature realization, and compact attribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength concept, independent replication, and demonstrated metrological consequences make B001 a complete achievement, while warning against treating a future redefinition of the second as necessary evidence.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts of this assessment. The decisive point is the physical mechanism allowing trapped atoms to retain the clock transition, not a prospective institutional outcome. This distinction also supplies the proper narrow boundary: recognize lattice clocks at the magic wavelength, rather than optical metrology as a whole."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson prefers B005 because the quantum spin Hall effect offers a direct theory-to-experiment chain for a new state of matter, but notes uncertainty over the experimental attribution and the boundary from the wider topological-materials field.",
        "response": "The comparison is useful because B005 has the sort of precise conceptual core I value. Yet its experimental and materials lineage remains harder to compress into the proposed three names. For B001, the conceptual and experimental roles of Katori and Ye are more readily distinguishable, provided that the citation stays confined to the magic-wavelength lattice-clock breakthrough."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson maintains that inflation and the quantum origin of cosmic structure have broad observational consequences, while reserving judgment because a distinctive direct signature and settled microscopic realization remain absent.",
        "response": "I recognize the extraordinary explanatory reach of B012. But the unresolved question of distinctive confirmation matters when comparing it with B001, whose underlying principle and reproducible experimental consequences are directly accessible. This is a comparison of scientific maturity and attribution, not of the number of supporters."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether priority review supports limiting the lattice-clock achievement to Katori and Ye rather than including other formative contributors.",
      "How narrowly the citation can isolate magic-wavelength optical lattice clocks from earlier optical-frequency metrology and subsequent performance advances.",
      "Whether the uneven realization of the full KTHNY transition sequence should ultimately keep B013 ahead as a theoretical prize."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct conflict or collaboration requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile. The shared Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson remains an institutional connection without a specific collaboration."
  }
}
